---
// Shopify and AI assistants: what each assistant does with a Shopify store, what Shopify publishes
// on the store's own domain, what our check counts for it (agentic commerce readiness, by the
// published rules) and what an owner can add. A page for one platform's owners, beside the eight
// guides and outside their contract (PLATFORM_PAGES in src/lib/guides.ts). Its words about Shopify
// and the assistants are in src/lib/shopify.ts, each with where we read it.
import Faqs from "../../components/Faqs.astro";
import GuideAnswer from "../../components/GuideAnswer.astro";
import GuideCheck from "../../components/GuideCheck.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { faqPage } from "../../lib/faq";
import { guideHref } from "../../lib/guides";
import {
  SHOPIFY,
  SHOPIFY_AS_OF,
  SHOPIFY_CHANNELS,
  SHOPIFY_CHECKED,
  SHOPIFY_DOORS,
  SHOPIFY_FAQS,
  SHOPIFY_SOURCES,
} from "../../lib/shopify";

const site = Astro.site?.toString() ?? "https://surfingdog.ai/";
const url = new URL(`/guides/${SHOPIFY.slug}/`, site).toString();
/** When this page was last read against Shopify's pages and the check's rules. */
const MODIFIED = "2026-10-12";
const ld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: SHOPIFY.heading,
    description: SHOPIFY.description,
    abstract: SHOPIFY.answer,
    datePublished: MODIFIED,
    dateModified: MODIFIED,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Surfing Dog", url: site },
    publisher: { "@id": `${site}#org` },
    isPartOf: { "@type": "CollectionPage", name: "Guides", url: new URL(guideHref(""), site).toString() },
    about: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Shopify" },
    image: new URL("/art/og-network-v1.png", site).toString(),
    mainEntityOfPage: url,
    url,
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: new URL(guideHref(""), site).toString() },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Shopify and AI assistants", item: url },
    ],
  },
  faqPage(SHOPIFY_FAQS, url),
];
const sources = Object.values(SHOPIFY_SOURCES);
const doorSources = [
  SHOPIFY_SOURCES.checkout,
  SHOPIFY_SOURCES.order,
  SHOPIFY_SOURCES.storefrontMcp,
  SHOPIFY_SOURCES.agentsMd,
];
---

<Layout title={`${SHOPIFY.title} · Surfing Dog`} description={SHOPIFY.description} type="article" ld={ld}>
  <article class="wrap wrap-read section is-head guide" aria-labelledby="g-title">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href={guideHref("")}>Guides</a> · Shopify</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="g-title">{SHOPIFY.heading}</h1>
    <GuideAnswer text={SHOPIFY.answer} />

    <div class="prose g-body">
      <h2 id="assistants">What each assistant does with your store</h2>
      <p>
        Shopify calls it agentic storefronts: your products reach AI assistants through Shopify Catalog. It is on by
        default for an eligible store, needs your terms of service, privacy policy and refund policy filled in, and each
        channel can be switched off in your admin. Orders arrive in your Shopify admin like any other, and fulfilment,
        returns and customer service stay with you (<a href={SHOPIFY_SOURCES.storefronts.href} rel="noopener"
          >{SHOPIFY_SOURCES.storefronts.label}</a
        >). What each one does, {SHOPIFY_AS_OF}:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          SHOPIFY_CHANNELS.map((c) => (
            <li>
              <b>{c.name}.</b> {c.does}{" "}
              <span class="src">
                ({c.sources.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && "; "}<a href={s.href} rel="noopener">{s.label}</a></>)})
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2 id="doors">What Shopify publishes on your domain</h2>
      <p>
        Shopify serves a UCP profile, its MCP server, a policies search and an llms.txt on your store's own address, with
        nothing for you to install.
      </p>
      <details class="more">
        <summary>The four addresses, and what each does</summary>
        <ul>
          {
            SHOPIFY_DOORS.map((d) => (
              <li>
                <code>{d.path}</code>: {d.what}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p class="src">
          ({doorSources.map((src, i) => <>{i > 0 && "; "}<a href={src.href} rel="noopener">{src.label}</a></>)})
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2 id="check">Agentic commerce readiness on Shopify: what our check counts</h2>
      <p>
        We read what your store publishes, and never place an order. Under the
        <a href="/v1/score-rules">published rules</a> (version 2, amended on 12 October 2026), a Shopify store is scored
        as a shop. When we can read your UCP profile and its server, ordering, paying, your catalogue and tracking count.
      </p>
      <details class="more">
        <summary>What counts, and from where</summary>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Order</b>: the UCP checkout, its <code>create_checkout</code> tool.</li>
          <li><b>Pay</b>: the payment handlers your UCP profile names.</li>
          <li><b>Catalogue and stock</b>: the UCP catalogue, such as <code>search_catalog</code>.</li>
          <li><b>Track</b>: the UCP order capability.</li>
          <li>
            <b>Who you are</b>: <code>LocalBusiness</code> markup with your name, address and opening hours; selling
            through UCP counts in place of the address.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <p>These don't come with Shopify's doors, and they hold most of the points left:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>A way to ask you something</b>, before buying or when something goes wrong. Since 12 October it counts for
          shops: about 14 of 100 points.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Changing, cancelling, returning and a receipt</b> for an order: about 8 points each. Shopify's order tool reads
          an order an agent placed; it doesn't change, cancel or return it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Cancelling a cart or a checkout</b> (<code>cancel_cart</code>, <code>cancel_checkout</code>): that isn't
          cancelling an order, so it earns nothing.
        </li>
        <li>Pages made for people, such as your contact form, chat widget and order status page.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="add">What you can add</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>A way to ask you something.</b> An A2A agent card, a <code>send_message</code> or
          <code>create_support_ticket</code> tool on an MCP server on your domain, a support service your UCP profile
          declares with its MCP server on your domain, or an agent inbox. Ask your helpdesk app whether it offers one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Order lookup, changes, cancelling, returns and receipts.</b> Tools such as <code>get_order_status</code>,
          <code>update_order</code>, <code>cancel_order</code>, <code>request_return</code> and <code>get_invoice</code>,
          on an MCP server or in an OpenAPI document on your domain; a lookup by order number and email is a simple start.
          Ask your returns or order-tracking app whether it offers one. A request that a person at your shop approves
          counts the same as one carried out at once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Your llms.txt, naming your doors.</b> If you replace Shopify's file with your own template, keep its UCP
          lines, and add a line for each door you add.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Surfing Dog Inbox (ours, open source)</b>, one option among apps for asking you something: free software
          (MIT) you run at your own subdomain, where agents write to you and you answer from its app, from your helpdesk
          through a bridge, or with your own AI. There's no hosted version yet. Orders placed in your Shopify checkout
          stay in Shopify, so changing or returning them, or a receipt, needs a door there (<a href="/inbox/"
            >how the inbox works</a
          >).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For any shop: <a href={guideHref("sell-through-ai-assistants")}>agentic commerce readiness</a> · <a
          href={guideHref("ai-assistant-messages-and-quotes")}>messages and quotes</a
        > · <a href={guideHref("llms-txt")}>what llms.txt does</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <GuideCheck
      examples={[{ href: "/leaderboard?can=order", label: "Businesses an agent can order from" }]}
      title="Check your store"
    />
    <Faqs faqs={SHOPIFY_FAQS} />
    <details class="sources">
      <summary>Sources, read {SHOPIFY_CHECKED}</summary>
      <ul>
        {
          sources.map((s) => (
            <li>
              <a class="link" href={s.href} rel="noopener">
                {s.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
        <li><a class="link" href="/v1/score-rules">Surfing Dog: the agentic score's rules</a></li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide {
    padding-bottom: var(--section-y);
  }
  .eyebrow a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .eyebrow a:hover,
  .eyebrow a:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  /* The same reading size as a guide's prose. */
  .g-body {
    font-size: var(--fs-body);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
  }
  .g-body > :global(p:first-child) {
    font-size: inherit;
    line-height: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .g-body h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-section);
  }
  .g-body ul {
    list-style: disc;
  }
  .g-body li::marker {
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .g-body b {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .g-body :not(pre) > code {
    padding: 0 4px;
    border-color: transparent;
    background: var(--row-bg);
    font-size: var(--fs-mono);
  }
  .src {
    font-size: var(--fs-fine);
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .more summary {
    width: fit-content;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .more summary:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .more > :global(ul) {
    margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  }
  .sources summary {
    width: fit-content;
    font-size: var(--fs-fine);
    color: var(--ink-3);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .sources summary:hover {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .sources ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-1);
    margin: var(--sp-2) 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    list-style: disc;
    font-size: var(--fs-fine);
  }
</style>
